The sine-Gordon model is integrable, and its S-matrix satisfies the bootstrap equations. The breather spectrum is generated recursively by fusing lower breathers into higher ones. This fusion hierarchy is the quantum analog of the classical breather solutions and is determined by the pole structure of the S-matrix.

### Step 1: Breather Spectrum (Recap)
The \(n\)-th breather \(B_n\) has mass

\[
M_n = 2 M \sin\left( \frac{n \pi \xi}{2} \right)
\]

where \(M\) is the classical soliton mass and \(\xi = \frac{\beta^2}{8\pi - \beta^2}\) is the coupling parameter.

The spectrum is limited to \(n < 1/\xi\), so there are finitely many stable breathers.

